"""ML & AI interview Q&A: (topic, level, frequency, question, answer). Companies are tagged by topic in ml_data."""

V, C, S = "Very common", "Common", "Sometimes"

QA = [
# ---------------- ML fundamentals ----------------
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", V, "What is the bias–variance trade-off?",
 "Error = bias² + variance + irreducible noise. **High bias** = model too simple, underfits (train and validation both bad). **High variance** = model too sensitive to the training sample, overfits (train good, validation bad). Increasing complexity lowers bias but raises variance; regularisation, more data and ensembles move you to the sweet spot."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", V, "What is overfitting and how do you prevent it?",
 "The model learns noise in the training data and fails to generalise — big gap between train and validation scores. Prevent with more data/augmentation, simpler models or fewer features, regularisation (L1/L2, dropout), early stopping, cross-validation for tuning, and ensembling (bagging)."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", V, "Supervised vs unsupervised vs reinforcement learning?",
 "**Supervised**: learn input→label mapping from labelled data (classification, regression). **Unsupervised**: find structure without labels (clustering, dimensionality reduction, anomalies). **Reinforcement**: an agent learns actions from rewards through trial and error. Also semi-supervised (few labels) and self-supervised (labels generated from the data, as in GPT/BERT)."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", V, "Why do we need a validation set and a test set?",
 "Tuning hyperparameters, features or early stopping on a dataset fits it a little, so its score becomes optimistic. The validation set (or cross-validation) absorbs that tuning; the test set is touched once at the end for an unbiased estimate of real-world performance."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", V, "Explain k-fold cross-validation. When should you NOT shuffle?",
 "Split data into k folds, train on k−1 and validate on the remaining one, rotating k times and averaging — a lower-variance estimate than one split. Use stratified folds for classification and group folds when rows share an entity. Don't shuffle time series: use forward-chaining (time-series split) so you never train on the future."),
("ML fundamentals", "Intermediate", V, "What is data leakage? Give examples.",
 "Information unavailable at prediction time leaks into training, inflating offline scores. Examples: a feature recorded after the outcome (account-closed date for churn), scaling/imputing/feature-selecting on the full dataset before splitting, random splits on time series, the same patient in train and test, duplicates across splits. Prevent with pipelines, time/group splits and asking 'would I know this at prediction time?'."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", C, "Parameters vs hyperparameters?",
 "Parameters are learned from data during training (weights, tree splits). Hyperparameters are set before training and control learning (learning rate, depth, k, regularisation strength) — tuned on validation data with grid/random/Bayesian search."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", C, "What is the curse of dimensionality?",
 "As features grow, the space grows exponentially, data becomes sparse and distances concentrate (nearest and farthest neighbours look alike), hurting kNN, clustering and kernels and requiring far more data. Remedies: feature selection, dimensionality reduction, regularisation, domain knowledge."),
("ML fundamentals", "Intermediate", C, "How do you handle missing data?",
 "Understand the mechanism (MCAR/MAR/MNAR). Options: drop rows/columns if few or useless; impute mean/median/mode or group-wise; model-based (KNN, iterative); add a missing-indicator because missingness can be informative; use models that handle NaN natively (XGBoost, LightGBM). Fit imputers on training folds only."),
("ML fundamentals", "Intermediate", V, "How do you deal with an imbalanced dataset?",
 "Use the right metrics (precision, recall, F1, PR-AUC — not accuracy); stratified splits; class weights or cost-sensitive loss; resampling the training set (undersampling, oversampling, SMOTE); threshold tuning on validation; focal loss; anomaly-detection framing; collect more minority data."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", C, "What is feature scaling and which algorithms need it?",
 "Bringing features to comparable ranges (standardisation z = (x−μ)/σ, min-max to [0,1], robust scaling). Needed for distance- and gradient-based methods: kNN, k-means, SVM, PCA, linear/logistic regression with regularisation, neural networks. Tree-based models don't need it."),
("ML fundamentals", "Intermediate", C, "Feature selection vs feature extraction?",
 "Selection keeps a subset of original features (filter: correlation/mutual information; wrapper: RFE; embedded: Lasso, tree importance) — interpretable. Extraction creates new features from combinations (PCA, LDA, autoencoders, embeddings) — compact but less interpretable."),
("ML fundamentals", "Intermediate", S, "What is the No Free Lunch theorem?",
 "Averaged over all possible problems, no learning algorithm beats another. Practically: model choice depends on the data and problem, so you try several models and validate, guided by domain knowledge and inductive biases."),
("ML fundamentals", "Basic", C, "Generative vs discriminative models?",
 "Discriminative models learn P(y|x) or a decision boundary directly (logistic regression, SVM, most neural classifiers). Generative models learn P(x, y) or P(x) and can generate data (Naive Bayes, GMMs, HMMs, VAEs, GANs, diffusion, LLMs). Discriminative models are usually more accurate for classification with enough data; generative ones handle missing data and sampling."),
("ML fundamentals", "Intermediate", C, "How do you choose a model for a new problem?",
 "Clarify the task, data size and type, latency and interpretability needs. Start with a baseline (majority/mean, simple rule), then a strong simple model — logistic/linear regression, then gradient-boosted trees for tabular; pretrained CNN/transformer for images/text. Compare with proper cross-validation and pick the simplest model that meets the requirement."),
# ---------------- statistics & maths ----------------
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", V, "Explain Bayes' theorem with an example.",
 "P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A)/P(B): update a prior with evidence. A test with 99% sensitivity and 5% false-positive rate for a disease with 1% prevalence gives P(disease|positive) ≈ 0.0099/(0.0099+0.0495) ≈ 17% — rare conditions stay unlikely after one positive test (base-rate fallacy)."),
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", V, "What is a p-value?",
 "The probability of observing data at least as extreme as yours if the null hypothesis were true. Small p (< α, e.g. 0.05) ⇒ reject the null. It is NOT the probability the null is true, and says nothing about effect size — report confidence intervals too."),
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", C, "Type I vs Type II error?",
 "Type I = false positive (reject a true null; rate α). Type II = false negative (miss a real effect; rate β). Power = 1 − β, increased by larger samples or larger effects. In classification terms: FP vs FN."),
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", C, "Explain the Central Limit Theorem.",
 "The mean of many independent samples is approximately normally distributed (with standard error σ/√n) regardless of the original distribution. It justifies confidence intervals and z/t-tests for averages such as conversion rates in A/B tests."),
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", C, "Correlation vs causation?",
 "Correlation measures co-movement; causation means changing X changes Y. Confounders, reverse causation or chance can create correlation. Establish causation with randomised experiments (A/B tests) or causal-inference methods controlling confounders."),
("Statistics & maths", "Intermediate", C, "How would you design an A/B test for a new recommendation model?",
 "Define the primary metric (e.g. watch time/CTR) and guardrails; compute sample size from baseline rate, minimum detectable effect, α and power; randomise users (not requests); run for full weekly cycles; don't peek; analyse with the right test and confidence intervals; check novelty effects and segment results."),
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", C, "Normalization vs standardization?",
 "Min-max normalization rescales to [0, 1] (sensitive to outliers; good for bounded data like pixels). Standardization subtracts the mean and divides by the std (mean 0, std 1; default for most models). Both are fitted on training data only."),
("Statistics & maths", "Intermediate", C, "What are eigenvalues/eigenvectors and their role in PCA?",
 "Av = λv: eigenvectors are directions a matrix only scales, eigenvalues are the scale factors. PCA eigen-decomposes the covariance matrix: eigenvectors = principal directions, eigenvalues = variance captured, so you keep the top-k eigenvectors."),
("Statistics & maths", "Intermediate", S, "Why is the derivative of the sigmoid a problem for deep networks?",
 "σ'(x) = σ(x)(1−σ(x)) ≤ 0.25 and ≈ 0 when saturated. Backprop multiplies these across layers, so gradients shrink exponentially (vanishing gradients) — one reason ReLU replaced sigmoid in hidden layers."),
("Statistics & maths", "Intermediate", C, "What is maximum likelihood estimation? How does it relate to common losses?",
 "MLE picks parameters that maximise the probability of the observed data. Minimising MSE is MLE under Gaussian noise; minimising cross-entropy is MLE for Bernoulli/categorical labels. Adding a prior gives MAP estimation, equivalent to L2 (Gaussian prior) or L1 (Laplace prior) regularisation."),
("Statistics & maths", "Intermediate", S, "What is KL divergence and where is it used?",
 "D_KL(P‖Q) = Σ P log(P/Q) measures how one distribution differs from another; ≥ 0, asymmetric. Used in VAEs (latent regularisation), knowledge distillation, RLHF/PPO (penalty to the reference model), and t-SNE."),
("Statistics & maths", "Basic", S, "Mean vs median — when to use which?",
 "The median is robust to outliers and skew (salaries, house prices, latency); the mean uses all values and suits symmetric data. For skewed data report the median and percentiles."),
# ---------------- supervised algorithms ----------------
("Supervised learning", "Basic", V, "Explain logistic regression. Why is it a linear classifier?",
 "It models log-odds as a linear function: p = σ(w·x + b), trained with cross-entropy (convex). The decision boundary p = 0.5 is w·x + b = 0 — a hyperplane — so it is linear in the features (unless you add non-linear features). Coefficients are interpretable as log-odds ratios."),
("Supervised learning", "Basic", V, "What are the assumptions of linear regression?",
 "Linearity, independent errors, homoscedasticity (constant error variance), normally distributed errors (for inference), and no severe multicollinearity. Check with residual plots, VIF and Q–Q plots."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "L1 vs L2 regularisation?",
 "L1 (Lasso) adds λΣ|w| and drives some weights exactly to zero → feature selection. L2 (Ridge/weight decay) adds λΣw², shrinking weights smoothly and handling correlated features well. Elastic Net mixes both. Geometrically, the L1 ball has corners on the axes where solutions land."),
("Supervised learning", "Basic", V, "How does a decision tree decide splits?",
 "Greedily: at each node try every feature and threshold, choose the split that maximally reduces impurity (Gini or entropy → information gain for classification; variance/MSE for regression), recurse until stopping criteria. Control overfitting with max_depth, min_samples_leaf or cost-complexity pruning."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "Bagging vs boosting?",
 "Bagging trains models in parallel on bootstrap samples and averages them → reduces variance (random forest). Boosting trains models sequentially, each correcting the previous errors → reduces bias (AdaBoost, gradient boosting). Bagging is robust to noise; boosting is usually more accurate but more sensitive to noisy labels and needs tuning."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "How does a random forest work and why is it better than one tree?",
 "Many deep trees, each trained on a bootstrap sample and considering a random subset of features at each split; predictions are averaged/voted. Bootstrap + feature randomness decorrelate trees, so averaging cuts variance massively while bias stays low. Also gives OOB estimates and feature importance."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "Explain gradient boosting and XGBoost.",
 "Start with a constant prediction; repeatedly fit a small tree to the negative gradient (residuals) of the loss and add it scaled by a learning rate. XGBoost adds second-order optimisation, L1/L2 regularisation on leaves, missing-value handling, column/row subsampling, parallel split finding and early stopping."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", C, "XGBoost vs LightGBM vs CatBoost?",
 "All are gradient-boosted trees. XGBoost: level-wise growth, mature, robust. LightGBM: histogram + leaf-wise growth, GOSS/EFB — fastest on big data, watch num_leaves for overfitting. CatBoost: native categorical handling with ordered target statistics and ordered boosting — great defaults with categorical data."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "Explain SVM and the kernel trick.",
 "SVM finds the maximum-margin hyperplane; soft margin with C trades margin vs violations (hinge loss). Its dual depends only on dot products, so replacing them with a kernel K(x, z) (RBF, polynomial) gives non-linear boundaries without computing high-dimensional features. Only support vectors define the solution."),
("Supervised learning", "Basic", C, "How does kNN work and how do you choose k?",
 "Predict by majority vote (or average) of the k closest training points. Small k → low bias, high variance; large k → smoother, more bias. Choose k by cross-validation; scale features; use KD-trees/ANN for speed; suffers in high dimensions."),
("Supervised learning", "Basic", C, "Why is Naive Bayes 'naive' and why does it still work?",
 "It assumes features are conditionally independent given the class. Despite this rarely holding, it ranks classes well because it only needs the argmax right, is robust with little data and high dimensions (text). Use Laplace smoothing and log probabilities."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", C, "How would you interpret a black-box model's prediction?",
 "Globally: permutation importance, partial dependence/ICE plots, surrogate models. Locally: SHAP values (additive feature contributions), LIME, counterfactual explanations. For CNNs: Grad-CAM/saliency. Validate explanations and remember they explain the model, not causality."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", S, "What is stacking?",
 "Train diverse base models, generate their out-of-fold predictions, and train a meta-model on those predictions to learn the best combination. Out-of-fold predictions prevent the meta-model from learning from overfit outputs."),
("Supervised learning", "Intermediate", S, "Why might gradient-boosted trees beat neural networks on tabular data?",
 "Tabular data has heterogeneous, often non-smooth features with irrelevant columns and moderate size. Trees handle mixed scales, missing values, sharp thresholds and interactions natively and are robust with little tuning, while neural nets need careful preprocessing and more data and have a smoothness bias."),
# ---------------- metrics ----------------
("Evaluation metrics", "Basic", V, "Precision vs recall — give examples where each matters more.",
 "Precision = TP/(TP+FP): of predicted positives, how many are right (spam filter — don't lose real mail). Recall = TP/(TP+FN): of actual positives, how many we catch (cancer screening, fraud). F1 balances them; choose the threshold from the relative costs."),
("Evaluation metrics", "Basic", V, "What is ROC-AUC? When is PR-AUC better?",
 "ROC plots TPR vs FPR over thresholds; AUC = probability a random positive scores above a random negative (0.5 random, 1 perfect), threshold-independent. With heavy imbalance, ROC-AUC can look great while precision is poor because FPR stays tiny; PR-AUC focuses on the positive class and is more informative."),
("Evaluation metrics", "Basic", V, "Why is accuracy misleading for imbalanced data?",
 "With 99% negatives, predicting 'negative' always gives 99% accuracy while catching zero positives. Use recall, precision, F1, PR-AUC, balanced accuracy or MCC."),
("Evaluation metrics", "Basic", C, "MAE vs RMSE vs R²?",
 "MAE averages absolute errors — robust, interpretable. RMSE squares errors — punishes large ones more. R² = fraction of variance explained vs predicting the mean (can be negative). Use MAPE/WAPE for relative error in forecasting (careful near zero)."),
("Evaluation metrics", "Intermediate", C, "Macro vs micro vs weighted F1?",
 "Macro: average per-class F1 equally — highlights small classes. Micro: pool all TP/FP/FN — dominated by large classes (equals accuracy for single-label multiclass). Weighted: per-class F1 weighted by support."),
("Evaluation metrics", "Intermediate", S, "What is model calibration and how do you check it?",
 "Calibrated probabilities match observed frequencies (of predictions at 0.8, ~80% are positive). Check with reliability diagrams, Brier score, ECE. Fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression on validation data. Matters for decisions using probabilities (risk, pricing)."),
("Evaluation metrics", "Intermediate", C, "How do you evaluate a ranking or recommendation model?",
 "Offline: precision@k, recall@k, MAP, MRR, NDCG on held-out interactions (time-based split), plus coverage/diversity. Online: A/B tests on CTR, watch time, conversion and long-term retention with guardrails."),
# ---------------- unsupervised ----------------
("Unsupervised learning", "Basic", V, "How does k-means work and how do you pick k?",
 "Initialise k centroids (k-means++), assign points to the nearest centroid, move centroids to cluster means, repeat until stable — minimises within-cluster squared distance. Pick k with the elbow method, silhouette score, gap statistic and business usefulness. Scale features first."),
("Unsupervised learning", "Intermediate", C, "k-means vs DBSCAN vs hierarchical vs GMM?",
 "k-means: fast, spherical similar-size clusters, needs k. DBSCAN: density-based, any shape, labels noise, no k but needs eps/min_samples. Hierarchical: dendrogram at all levels, O(n²). GMM: soft probabilistic assignments, elliptical clusters, choose k by BIC."),
("Unsupervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "Explain PCA. What are its limitations?",
 "Standardise, compute the covariance matrix, take eigenvectors with the largest eigenvalues, project data onto them — keeps maximum variance in fewer dimensions. Limitations: linear, variance ≠ importance for the target, sensitive to scaling/outliers, components hard to interpret."),
("Unsupervised learning", "Intermediate", C, "PCA vs t-SNE vs UMAP?",
 "PCA: linear, fast, deterministic, preserves global variance, can transform new data. t-SNE: non-linear, preserves local neighbourhoods for visualisation, slow, distances between clusters meaningless. UMAP: faster, keeps more global structure, can transform new data."),
("Unsupervised learning", "Intermediate", C, "How do you detect anomalies without labels?",
 "Statistical rules (z-score, IQR, Mahalanobis), Isolation Forest, Local Outlier Factor, One-Class SVM, density models (GMM/KDE), autoencoder reconstruction error trained on normal data, forecasting residuals for time series. Set thresholds from score percentiles and validate on any known anomalies."),
("Unsupervised learning", "Intermediate", V, "How do recommendation systems work? Content-based vs collaborative filtering?",
 "Content-based recommends items similar to what the user liked using item features (works for new items, can be narrow). Collaborative filtering uses interaction patterns — similar users/items or matrix factorisation into latent factors (no item features needed, cold-start problem). Production systems use hybrid two-stage retrieval + ranking."),
("Unsupervised learning", "Intermediate", C, "How do you solve cold start in recommenders?",
 "New users: popularity/trending, onboarding preferences, context (location, device), quick exploration via bandits. New items: content features/embeddings (text, image), boosted exploration. Feature-based models (two-tower) handle both naturally."),
# ---------------- deep learning ----------------
("Deep learning", "Basic", V, "What is backpropagation?",
 "Applying the chain rule backwards through the network's computational graph to get the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, reusing intermediate results; then an optimiser updates the weights. Autograd frameworks automate it."),
("Deep learning", "Basic", V, "Why do neural networks need non-linear activation functions?",
 "Without them any stack of layers collapses to a single linear transformation, so the network can only learn linear functions. Non-linearities (ReLU, GELU, tanh) let depth represent complex functions."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", V, "What are vanishing and exploding gradients and how do you fix them?",
 "Gradients are products of many layer Jacobians; they shrink or blow up exponentially with depth or sequence length. Fixes: ReLU-family activations, He/Xavier initialisation, batch/layer normalisation, residual connections, LSTM/GRU gates, gradient clipping, smaller learning rates."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", V, "Explain dropout. Why turn it off at inference?",
 "During training, randomly zero activations with probability p and scale the rest by 1/(1−p); prevents co-adaptation and acts like an ensemble of thinned networks. At inference we want deterministic predictions approximating the ensemble average, so dropout is disabled (model.eval())."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", V, "Batch normalization vs layer normalization?",
 "BatchNorm normalises each feature across the batch (needs reasonable batch sizes, uses running stats at inference) — standard in CNNs. LayerNorm normalises across features within each example — batch-size independent, standard in transformers and RNNs."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", V, "SGD vs Adam? What is AdamW?",
 "SGD (+momentum) uses one global learning rate; generalises very well with tuning (vision). Adam adapts per-parameter steps from running means of gradients and squared gradients — fast, robust defaults. AdamW decouples weight decay from the adaptive update, regularising correctly; standard for transformers."),
("Deep learning", "Basic", C, "Batch size and learning rate — how do they interact?",
 "Larger batches give less noisy gradients and better hardware use; you can usually raise the learning rate roughly proportionally (with warm-up). Very large batches may generalise worse; small batches add helpful noise. Use gradient accumulation to emulate big batches."),
("Deep learning", "Basic", V, "How does a CNN work? What do convolution and pooling do?",
 "Convolutions slide small learnable filters across the image, detecting local patterns with shared weights (few parameters, translation equivariance). Pooling downsamples, adding robustness to small shifts. Stacked layers build a hierarchy from edges to objects; a final head classifies."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", C, "Compute the output size and parameters of a conv layer: 32×32×3 input, 16 filters 5×5, stride 1, padding 2.",
 "Output = (32 + 2·2 − 5)/1 + 1 = 32 → 32×32×16. Parameters = (5·5·3 + 1)·16 = 1,216."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", V, "What is transfer learning and when do you freeze layers?",
 "Reuse a model pretrained on large data as initialisation. With little data similar to the pretraining data, freeze the backbone and train a new head; with more or different data, fine-tune more layers with a small learning rate (discriminative rates, gradual unfreezing)."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", C, "Explain ResNet's residual connections.",
 "Each block outputs x + F(x): an identity skip path lets gradients flow directly and makes it easy to learn near-identity mappings, solving the degradation problem of very deep networks — enabling 100+ layer models."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", C, "RNN vs LSTM vs GRU?",
 "Plain RNNs carry a hidden state but forget beyond ~10–20 steps due to vanishing gradients. LSTMs add a cell state with forget/input/output gates and additive updates for long memory. GRUs merge gates (update/reset), fewer parameters, similar performance. Transformers now dominate long sequences."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", C, "What is an autoencoder? Give two uses.",
 "An encoder compresses input to a bottleneck code and a decoder reconstructs it; trained on reconstruction loss without labels. Uses: dimensionality reduction, denoising/image reconstruction, anomaly detection (high reconstruction error), pretraining; VAEs add a probabilistic latent space for generation."),
("Deep learning", "Advanced", C, "How do GANs work and what are their training problems?",
 "A generator maps noise to fake samples; a discriminator classifies real vs fake; they play a minimax game. Problems: mode collapse, instability, vanishing discriminator gradients. Fixes: WGAN-GP, spectral norm, careful architectures, two-timescale learning rates."),
("Deep learning", "Advanced", S, "What is a Siamese network and triplet loss?",
 "Two/three inputs pass through the same weight-shared encoder; the distance between embeddings measures similarity. Triplet loss max(0, d(a,p) − d(a,n) + margin) pulls same-identity pairs together and pushes others apart — used for face verification, one-shot learning and duplicate detection."),
("Deep learning", "Intermediate", C, "Your training loss doesn't decrease. How do you debug?",
 "Check data and labels (shuffle bug, wrong preprocessing), overfit a single small batch first, verify the loss/output activation pairing (logits vs probabilities), try a lower/higher learning rate, check gradients for NaN/zero, initialisation and normalisation, and remove regularisation until the model can fit."),
("Deep learning", "Advanced", S, "How do diffusion models generate images?",
 "Training: add Gaussian noise to images at random timesteps and train a network (U-Net/DiT) to predict the noise. Generation: start from pure noise and iteratively denoise; condition on text via cross-attention and classifier-free guidance; latent diffusion runs this in a VAE latent space."),
# ---------------- computer vision ----------------
("Computer vision", "Intermediate", V, "Explain IoU, NMS and mAP in object detection.",
 "IoU = overlap/union of two boxes. NMS keeps the highest-scoring box and removes others of the same class overlapping above an IoU threshold. mAP = mean over classes of average precision (area under PR curve) at IoU thresholds (0.5 or 0.5:0.95)."),
("Computer vision", "Intermediate", C, "One-stage vs two-stage detectors?",
 "Two-stage (Faster R-CNN): region proposals then classification/refinement — accurate, slower. One-stage (YOLO, SSD, RetinaNet): dense predictions in one pass — real-time. Transformer detectors (DETR) predict sets directly without NMS."),
("Computer vision", "Intermediate", C, "Semantic vs instance vs panoptic segmentation? Why U-Net?",
 "Semantic: class per pixel; instance: separate masks per object; panoptic: both. U-Net's encoder captures context, decoder restores resolution, and skip connections pass fine details — accurate boundaries with little data."),
("Computer vision", "Basic", C, "Which data augmentations would you use for medical X-rays?",
 "Small rotations, scaling, translation, brightness/contrast, mild noise/blur — realistic acquisition variations. Avoid flips if laterality matters and anything that changes diagnostic content; validate augmentations improve validation recall."),
("Computer vision", "Advanced", S, "How does a Vision Transformer work?",
 "Split the image into patches (16×16), linearly embed them, add position embeddings and a class token, then apply a transformer encoder. Lacking CNN inductive biases, it needs large-scale pretraining (or distillation/augmentation) but scales extremely well."),
("Computer vision", "Intermediate", S, "How do you measure super-resolution quality and why do PSNR and visual quality disagree?",
 "PSNR/SSIM measure pixel fidelity; MSE-trained models maximise them but produce blurry averages. Perceptual/adversarial losses (ESRGAN) give sharper realistic textures with lower PSNR — so also use LPIPS and human evaluation."),
# ---------------- NLP & transformers ----------------
("NLP & transformers", "Basic", V, "TF-IDF vs word embeddings vs contextual embeddings?",
 "TF-IDF: sparse counts weighted by rarity, no semantics. Word embeddings (word2vec, GloVe): dense vectors capturing similarity, one vector per word. Contextual embeddings (BERT): vectors depend on the sentence, resolving ambiguity ('river bank' vs 'bank loan')."),
("NLP & transformers", "Advanced", V, "Explain self-attention.",
 "Each token produces query, key and value vectors. Attention weights = softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k) compare each query to all keys; the output is the weighted sum of values. It lets every token gather information from every other token in parallel; multi-head attention learns several relation types."),
("NLP & transformers", "Advanced", V, "Explain the transformer architecture.",
 "Token embeddings + positional information → stacked blocks of multi-head self-attention and position-wise feed-forward layers, each with residual connections and layer norm. Encoder–decoder versions add masked self-attention and cross-attention in the decoder. Parallel, captures long-range dependencies, scales well."),
("NLP & transformers", "Advanced", V, "BERT vs GPT vs T5?",
 "BERT: encoder-only, bidirectional, masked-LM pretraining — understanding tasks (classification, NER, embeddings). GPT: decoder-only, causal next-token prediction — generation, few-shot, chat. T5: encoder–decoder, span corruption, text-to-text for translation/summarisation."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", C, "Why scale attention scores by √d_k?",
 "Dot products grow with dimension (variance ≈ d_k), pushing softmax into saturation with tiny gradients. Dividing by √d_k keeps scores at unit variance."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", C, "Why do transformers need positional encodings?",
 "Self-attention is permutation-invariant — it sees a set of tokens. Positional encodings (sinusoidal, learned, RoPE, ALiBi) inject order so 'dog bites man' differs from 'man bites dog'."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", C, "What is subword tokenisation (BPE/WordPiece) and why use it?",
 "Words are split into frequent pieces learned from data (BPE merges frequent pairs; WordPiece maximises likelihood). It keeps the vocabulary manageable, handles rare/unseen words and morphology, and keeps sequences shorter than characters."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", V, "How do you fine-tune BERT for text classification?",
 "Tokenise with its tokenizer (truncation/padding), add a classification head on [CLS], train end-to-end with a small learning rate (2e-5–5e-5) for 2–4 epochs with warm-up and weight decay, evaluate each epoch, early stop. Hugging Face Trainer handles this."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", C, "Greedy vs beam search vs top-k vs top-p sampling?",
 "Greedy picks the most likely token (deterministic, repetitive). Beam search keeps the k best sequences (translation/summarisation). Sampling with temperature adds diversity; top-k restricts to k tokens; top-p (nucleus) to the smallest set covering probability p — adaptive."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", S, "What is perplexity?",
 "exp(average negative log-likelihood per token) on held-out text — the effective number of choices per step. Lower is better; only comparable with the same tokenizer and data."),
("NLP & transformers", "Basic", C, "How would you build a sentiment classifier for product reviews?",
 "Baseline TF-IDF (word + char n-grams) + logistic regression; then fine-tune DistilBERT/DeBERTa if you have thousands of labels, or prompt an LLM if labels are scarce. Handle negation/sarcasm, imbalance, evaluate macro-F1 and inspect errors; consider aspect-based sentiment."),
("NLP & transformers", "Intermediate", S, "How would you detect duplicate questions (Quora Question Pairs)?",
 "Pairwise classification: features like TF-IDF cosine, word overlap, length differences + GBM baseline; better, a Siamese sentence-transformer (bi-encoder) for embedding similarity and a cross-encoder (BERT on both questions) for accuracy; tune the threshold on validation; use ANN search to find candidates at scale."),
# ---------------- LLMs & GenAI ----------------
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", V, "How are LLMs like ChatGPT trained?",
 "Pre-training on trillions of tokens with next-token prediction; supervised fine-tuning on instruction–response demonstrations; preference alignment with RLHF (reward model + PPO) or DPO, sometimes AI feedback (constitutional AI); plus reasoning RL, tool use and safety training."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", V, "What is hallucination and how do you reduce it?",
 "Fluent but false or unsupported output, because models generate plausible text rather than verify facts. Reduce with RAG grounding + citations, tools (search, calculators), lower temperature, prompts that allow 'I don't know', verification/self-consistency steps, fine-tuning on grounded data, and faithfulness evaluation."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", V, "What is RAG and why use it instead of fine-tuning?",
 "Retrieval-augmented generation retrieves relevant document chunks and puts them in the prompt so the LLM answers from them with citations. It adds fresh/private knowledge without retraining, is easy to update, supports permissions and reduces hallucination. Fine-tuning changes behaviour/format, not reliable fact storage."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", V, "Design a RAG system — what are the key components and failure points?",
 "Ingestion (parsing, chunking with overlap, metadata, embeddings, vector DB), retrieval (hybrid BM25 + dense, filters, re-ranking), generation (grounded prompt, citations, abstain), evaluation (recall@k, faithfulness, answer correctness), monitoring. Failures: bad chunking, wrong retrieval, context overload, stale index, permission leaks, prompt injection in documents."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", C, "What is LoRA / QLoRA?",
 "LoRA freezes pretrained weights and learns low-rank updates ΔW = BA (rank 4–64) in chosen layers — <1% trainable parameters, small adapters, mergeable at inference. QLoRA loads the base model in 4-bit NF4 and trains LoRA adapters on top, fitting very large models on one GPU."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", C, "RLHF vs DPO?",
 "RLHF trains a reward model on human preference pairs, then optimises the policy with PPO under a KL penalty to the reference model. DPO optimises the same preference objective directly with a classification-style loss on chosen/rejected pairs and a frozen reference — no reward model or RL loop; simpler and stable."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", C, "What is prompt engineering? Name techniques.",
 "Designing inputs for reliable outputs: clear instructions and roles, delimiters for context, few-shot examples, chain-of-thought / reasoning, decomposition and prompt chaining, structured output schemas, allowing abstention, self-consistency. Evaluate prompts on a test set."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", C, "What is an AI agent? How do you make one reliable?",
 "An LLM in a loop that reasons, calls tools via function calling and observes results until a goal is met (ReAct, plan-and-execute). Reliability: narrow well-described tools, schema validation, step limits, sandboxing, least privilege, human approval for irreversible actions, tracing and task-based evaluation; prefer explicit workflows (e.g. LangGraph) where possible."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", C, "What is prompt injection and how do you defend against it?",
 "Malicious instructions in user input or retrieved content (web pages, emails, documents) hijack the model. Defend in depth: treat external content as untrusted data, least-privilege tools, approval gates, output/tool-call validation, input classifiers, keep secrets out of context, monitor and red-team."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", S, "What is the KV cache?",
 "During autoregressive generation, keys and values of previous tokens are stored so each new token only computes its own projections — linear instead of quadratic recomputation. It uses lots of memory (layers × heads × tokens), motivating GQA, PagedAttention (vLLM) and KV quantisation."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", C, "How do you evaluate an LLM application?",
 "Golden test set of real inputs with references/rubrics; task metrics (exact match, F1, pass@k), LLM-as-judge calibrated to human ratings, RAG metrics (faithfulness, context recall), safety tests, latency and cost; run on every prompt/model change; online A/B tests and user feedback."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", C, "How do you reduce LLM latency and cost?",
 "Smaller models or routing easy queries to them, prompt trimming and caching, response caching, streaming, batching, quantisation and efficient serving (vLLM, speculative decoding), limiting output tokens, distillation/fine-tuning a small model for a narrow task."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Advanced", S, "What is a mixture-of-experts (MoE) model?",
 "Feed-forward layers are replaced by many experts with a router selecting the top-k per token, so total parameters are huge but compute per token is small. Challenges: load balancing (auxiliary losses), memory to hold all experts, communication across GPUs."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Intermediate", S, "Explain CLIP and zero-shot classification.",
 "CLIP trains image and text encoders contrastively on image–caption pairs so matches have high cosine similarity. Zero-shot: embed captions like 'a photo of a {class}' and pick the class most similar to the image embedding."),
("LLMs & GenAI", "Basic", C, "What does temperature do in an LLM?",
 "It divides the logits before softmax: low temperature sharpens the distribution (more deterministic — code, extraction), high temperature flattens it (more diverse — creative writing). T → 0 approaches greedy decoding."),
# ---------------- MLOps & deployment ----------------
("MLOps & deployment", "Intermediate", V, "How do you deploy an ML model to production?",
 "Package the full pipeline (preprocessing + model) with pinned dependencies, serve via a batch job or an API (FastAPI/TorchServe/Triton) in Docker, roll out with shadow/canary/A-B tests, monitor latency, errors, data drift and model metrics, and keep a model registry and rollback path."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Intermediate", V, "What is data drift vs concept drift? How do you monitor them?",
 "Data drift: input distributions change. Concept drift: the input→target relationship changes. Monitor feature distributions (PSI, KS tests), prediction distributions, and model metrics as labels arrive; alert, investigate and retrain."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Intermediate", C, "What is training–serving skew?",
 "Differences between how features/data are processed in training and in serving (different code paths, stale data, different defaults) causing worse production performance. Prevent with shared feature code, feature stores, logging served features and comparing distributions."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Intermediate", C, "Batch vs real-time inference?",
 "Batch precomputes predictions on a schedule — simpler, cheaper (daily churn scores). Real-time serves each request with low latency when inputs are only known at request time (fraud check, search). Hybrids precompute candidates/features and re-rank online."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Advanced", C, "How do you make a model smaller and faster?",
 "Quantisation (fp16/int8/4-bit), pruning (structured for real speed-ups), knowledge distillation to a smaller student, efficient architectures (MobileNet, DistilBERT), export to ONNX/TensorRT/TFLite, batching and caching; measure accuracy/latency on target hardware."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Intermediate", C, "What is MLOps? Name key tools.",
 "Practices for reliably building, deploying and maintaining ML: experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B), data/model versioning (DVC, registry), pipelines (Airflow, Kubeflow), feature stores (Feast), CI/CD with data and model tests, serving and monitoring (Evidently, Prometheus)."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Basic", S, "How would you serve a scikit-learn model with FastAPI?",
 "Save the pipeline with joblib, load it once at startup, define Pydantic request/response models, implement POST /predict and GET /health, test with TestClient, run with uvicorn in Docker behind a load balancer, add logging and monitoring."),
("MLOps & deployment", "Advanced", V, "How do you approach an ML system design interview?",
 "Clarify goals, scale and latency → frame as ML with labels and offline/online metrics → data and labelling → features → baseline and model choice → serving architecture (retrieval → ranking → re-ranking) → evaluation and A/B testing → monitoring, retraining, fairness and failure modes. Talk trade-offs throughout."),
# ---------------- responsible AI ----------------
("Responsible AI", "Intermediate", C, "How do you detect and mitigate bias in a model?",
 "Measure per-group metrics (selection rate, TPR/FPR, calibration) and fairness criteria (demographic parity, equalised odds, disparate impact); check proxy features; mitigate via re-sampling/re-weighting, fairness constraints, threshold adjustment, better data; document with model cards; keep humans in the loop for high-stakes decisions."),
("Responsible AI", "Intermediate", S, "What is human-in-the-loop ML and active learning?",
 "Humans label, review or approve where the model is uncertain or stakes are high. Active learning picks the most informative unlabeled examples (uncertainty, diversity) for labelling, cutting labelling cost; review queues route low-confidence predictions to people, whose decisions feed back into training."),
("Responsible AI", "Basic", S, "What privacy issues arise in ML and how do you address them?",
 "Personal data in training sets, memorisation/leakage of PII, re-identification, regulatory compliance (GDPR, India's DPDP Act). Address with data minimisation, anonymisation/pseudonymisation, access control, PII redaction, differential privacy, federated learning and retention policies."),
]
